#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cccebbd-5c63-5284-a6b1-eaba281612f4" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548825} :answer-field/id #uuid "c876964c-ac3b-5bc2-8e7a-39e0baa2db2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ba89d20-58fc-5947-9d86-d842de2f0086" :question/math-academy-id "q-344587" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f88b6c506d909cce64eef23c71acaed15e382c55571f02048e0c9c616d75544e.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $8,$ as shown above. Find the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$ \n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 256-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $12$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(8\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd8d346-74cd-5ecf-86c1-b5a78ea3f6db" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Perimeter of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d0883d-37ec-5524-9e37-0ebd4d8a596b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1+4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548397} {:answer/id #uuid "602e5323-47b5-567c-ad05-2e0f14c6bd42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548535} {:answer/id #uuid "81a1d39e-ffa3-5851-9280-ef0749290fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548602} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdb68bd-b484-51c4-a434-8a6450e1553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548728} {:answer/id #uuid "dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548780} :answer-field/id #uuid "814e03a2-fcae-503e-ad2a-4cd54263a43d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16b31b45-83e0-56eb-b7ca-34f65af6e8dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51320" :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebe962cc3394e0839d77fba922a5a7f56f31201f6926ef8b4236c1f82edd2e90.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10+l+\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =50 \\\\ l & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ | $5\\sqrt{2}$ | $(5\\sqrt{2},10)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $8$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\sqrt{2}) & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10+10\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd8d346-74cd-5ecf-86c1-b5a78ea3f6db" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Perimeter of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548395} {:answer/id #uuid "35dfa7d4-3b02-58c0-ad0c-6812b20c5dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548460} {:answer/id #uuid "b08dd26b-63ed-5c34-a8e4-74444069711e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548703} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eabd82-920e-5aa0-b2c1-c63c5538278b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548740} {:answer/id #uuid "ef21318a-633e-5e82-8e9e-01f49a6a7ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548395} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d676937-e9eb-5b1f-9a5a-f6981fa3e2b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e33bb576-2a78-5fb1-8965-49bc676e2392" :question/math-academy-id "q-84644" :question/problem "![](images/64/643d8218715b65def0d9fd274c50b18c76cfbea35cd3dc70f3cdbb5c5b16b6f8.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$2,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4+l+\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =8 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $3$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\sqrt{2}) & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-8} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd8d346-74cd-5ecf-86c1-b5a78ea3f6db" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Perimeter of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b31079-d767-507c-b7c2-8e2d5e35817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548431} {:answer/id #uuid "2877d8e6-e960-54e3-9caf-592270a7f040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548435} {:answer/id #uuid "65db8df0-61db-5c03-a942-ca2140d41e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(4+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548549} {:answer/id #uuid "b7211e4d-0ac5-51f4-aaeb-258dc346a3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548719} {:answer/id #uuid "f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548854} :answer-field/id #uuid "280fb089-0f58-509d-8499-e6519dccfdda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9e9c021-fc29-5d89-a4ff-166a4f01dd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-84649" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5\\sqrt{2}.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/29/296e866a3104f0d2a7891726274578bcdffb84853ce896e8723e2b742d22ac27.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =200-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =200 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ l & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,10)$ | $10$ | $(10,10\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $11$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+10 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+20 \\\\  & =10(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cccebbd-5c63-5284-a6b1-eaba281612f4" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ba2075-ff36-56d5-92fd-c143a5d90b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548506} {:answer/id #uuid "5319f4ad-15cf-555e-aa17-f39d7212a5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548511} {:answer/id #uuid "904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548638} {:answer/id #uuid "d92ad90d-da23-5fff-85e4-1da26bf2a05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548777} {:answer/id #uuid "e2149821-7718-5f16-85d2-5599e78ce659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548638} :answer-field/id #uuid "04564310-aef1-5572-80f0-0e88d49a5152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63d7b221-5dbf-5fb1-b55e-24803c54cfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-86380" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $10\\sqrt{2}.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, find the maximum possible area of the triangle." :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/17/172826a7fa27ac8957f120e15622cf0c96b0e8df66e5f6fd3d7d99ab0b32c5f0.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $20\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<20\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 800-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{800}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =400 \\\\ l & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,20)$ | $20$ | $(20,20\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $25$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(20) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot \\sqrt{800-{(20)}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{800-400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
